{
  "question": "Is the statistically significant negative drift observed in pendulum_lab readings (day 18 trial 156) indicative of a genuine systematic change in the pendulum period, or is it an artefact of environmental or procedural factors?",
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "label": "H1: Apparent drift is random fluctuation or unmodeled environmental variation (e.g., temperature, air pressure) that coincidentally lowered the pendulum_lab reading on day 18.",
      "probability": 0.25,
      "rationale": "The baseline dataset (days 1‑10) shows considerable natural variability; a single low outlier could arise by chance, especially if subtle environmental changes were not recorded.",
      "evidenceFor": ["676087"],
      "evidenceAgainst": []
    },
    {
      "label": "H2: Temperature‑related systematic effect on the pendulum_lab apparatus caused a genuine speed‑up of the swing, lowering the beat count.",
      "probability": 0.20,
      "rationale": "Temperature can alter pendulum length and air density, both affecting period. The isolated drop in pendulum_lab, without a corresponding change in pendulum_obs, suggests a local thermal influence.",
      "evidenceFor": ["676087"],
      "evidenceAgainst": []
    },
    {
      "label": "H3: Mechanical aging or tension change (e.g., cable stretch, support wear) in the pendulum_lab has progressively shortened the effective length, producing a real decrease in period.",
      "probability": 0.35,
      "rationale": "A z‑score of –2.18 indicates the post‑baseline mean is unlikely under the baseline variance. Mechanical drift is a common long‑term effect for pendulums and would explain a systematic negative shift.",
      "evidenceFor": ["676087"],
      "evidenceAgainst": []
    },
    {
      "label": "H4: A data‑processing or calibration artifact introduced at or after trial 156 caused a bias in the recorded pendulum_lab values.",
      "probability": 0.10,
      "rationale": "Changes in analysis scripts, rounding, or sensor calibration could produce an artificial offset, especially if only this instrument shows the drift.",
      "evidenceFor": ["676087"],
      "evidenceAgainst": []
    }
  ],
  "residual": 0.10,
  "summaryOfChange": "The previous review focused on resonator_obs drift, but the most statistically significant anomaly in the current dataset is the negative drift of pendulum_lab (z = –2.18, driven by the day 18 reading 1.6575 beats, event 676087). Consequently the central question has been reframed to address this pendulum drift. Probabilities have been redistributed to reflect a higher plausibility of a genuine mechanical or temperature‑driven change (H2/H3) versus random fluctuation (H1) or data artefacts (H4), with a residual allowance for unforeseen factors."
}

